3. Causal Graph Topology

3.1 Vertices

The TLSACE causal graph contains nine vertices:

V = \{C, O, G, R, E, A_d, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, B, H\}

where C is cognitive capability, O is objective compatibility with human constraints, G is governance intensity, R is resource budget, E is experimental environment, A_d is granted autonomy, A_{\mathrm{ex}} is exercised autonomy, B is exploration behavior, and H is multidimensional cooperation.

3.2 Directed edges

The directed edges representing functional dependence are:

\begin{aligned}
&G \to A_d \\
&A_d \to A_{\mathrm{ex}}, \quad C \to A_{\mathrm{ex}}, \quad R \to A_{\mathrm{ex}} \\
&C \to B, \quad O \to B, \quad G \to B, \quad R \to B, \quad E \to B, \quad A_{\mathrm{ex}} \to B \\
&C \to H, \quad O \to H, \quad G \to H, \quad E \to H, \quad A_{\mathrm{ex}} \to H
\end{aligned}

These edges encode the structural assumptions of the model. Governance determines granted autonomy. Granted autonomy, capability, and resources jointly determine exercised autonomy. Exploration and cooperation are downstream of the layered structure.

3.3 Acyclicity

The graph is acyclic. There exists no directed path from any vertex back to itself. This is verified by the topological ordering:

G \prec A_d \prec A_{\mathrm{ex}} \prec B, \quad
C \prec A_{\mathrm{ex}} \prec H, \quad
O \prec H, \quad
R \prec B, \quad
E \prec B

No edge points from a later vertex to an earlier one in this ordering.

