5. Theorems and Proofs

5.1 Theorem 1: Collider bias when conditioning on exercised autonomy

Statement. Let \text{allPaths} be the set of all directed paths in the TLSACE graph. Let the witness path be

\text{colliderBackdoorPath} = [C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H]

If \text{colliderBackdoorPath} \in \text{allPaths}, then

\neg \, \text{DSeparated}([C], [H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \text{allPaths})

That is, conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} fails to d-separate C and H.

Proof. Assume, for contradiction, that \text{DSeparated}([C], [H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \text{allPaths}) holds. Then for every path in \text{allPaths} with endpoints C and H, the path is blocked given Z = [A_{\mathrm{ex}}].

Apply the definition to the witness path:

\text{IsPathBlocked}([C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{true}

Evaluate the first triplet (C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d). The edges are C \to A_{\mathrm{ex}} and A_d \to A_{\mathrm{ex}}, so the triplet is a collider at A_{\mathrm{ex}}. Since A_{\mathrm{ex}} \in Z, the collider is open, and

\text{IsTripletBlocked}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{false}

Therefore, the recursive evaluation of \text{IsPathBlocked} reduces to

\text{IsPathBlocked}([C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}])
= \text{IsPathBlocked}([A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}])

This contradicts the assumption that the path is blocked. Hence,

\neg \, \text{DSeparated}([C], [H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \text{allPaths})

\square

Interpretation. Conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} opens the collider path C \rightarrow A_{\mathrm{ex}} \leftarrow A_d \leftarrow G \rightarrow H, creating a spurious association between C and G. Estimating the effect of C on H while conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} therefore produces endogenous selection bias.

5.2 Theorem 2: Backdoor adjustment with \{G, O, E\}

Statement. Let \text{backdoorZ} = [G, O, E]. The non-causal path

\text{nonCausalPath} = [C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H]

is blocked given \text{backdoorZ}:

\text{IsPathBlocked}([C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H], [G, O, E]) = \text{true}

Proof. Evaluate the first triplet (C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d). The edges are C \to A_{\mathrm{ex}} and A_d \to A_{\mathrm{ex}}, so the triplet is a collider at A_{\mathrm{ex}}. Since A_{\mathrm{ex}} \notin \{G, O, E\}, the collider is closed, and

\text{IsTripletBlocked}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, [G, O, E]) = \text{true}

Therefore,

\text{IsPathBlocked}([C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H], [G, O, E]) = \text{true}

\square

Interpretation. Conditioning on \{G, O, E\} blocks the non-causal path because G forms a fork A_d \leftarrow G \rightarrow H, and G \in Z. The collider at A_{\mathrm{ex}} remains closed because A_{\mathrm{ex}} \notin Z. Hence, \{G, O, E\} satisfies the backdoor criterion for estimating the total effect of C on H.

5.3 Theorem 3: Sequential ignorability for mediation

Statement. For mediation analysis via A_{\mathrm{ex}}, the direct effect of A_d on H is identified given the adjustment set \{C, G, E, A_{\mathrm{ex}}\}:

\text{DSeparated}([A_d], [H], [C, G, E, A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \text{allPaths})

Proof. The adjustment set \{C, G, E, A_{\mathrm{ex}}\} contains all parents of A_{\mathrm{ex}} except R, which is assumed to affect H only through B. Conditioning on C, G, and E blocks the backdoor paths from A_d to H. Conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} blocks the direct path A_{\mathrm{ex}} \to H. No unblocked backdoor path remains. \square

Interpretation. The sequential ignorability assumption holds when the mediator A_{\mathrm{ex}} is ignorable given observed confounders, and the outcome H is ignorable given the mediator and confounders. This is a strong assumption that cannot be verified from data alone.

