Appendix C. Full Proof of Theorem 1: Collider Bias

Theorem 1 (Collider bias when conditioning on exercised autonomy). Let

\pi_{\text{collider}} = [C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H]

be a path in the TLSACE graph. If \pi_{\text{collider}} is in the path set \Pi, then

\neg \, \text{DSeparated}([C], [H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \Pi)

Preliminary definitions.

\begin{aligned}
\text{DirectedEdge}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) &= \text{True} \\
\text{DirectedEdge}(A_d, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) &= \text{True} \\
\text{DirectedEdge}(G, A_d) &= \text{True} \\
\text{DirectedEdge}(G, H) &= \text{True}
\end{aligned}

Lemma C.1 (Triplet (C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d) is a collider). The triplet formed by the first three vertices of \pi_{\text{collider}} is classified as a collider.

Proof. We verify the condition for TripletType.Collider:

\text{DirectedEdge}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) = \text{True}

\text{DirectedEdge}(A_d, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) = \text{True}

The first branch of classifyTriplet checks \text{DirectedEdge}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) \wedge \text{DirectedEdge}(A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d), which is \text{True} \wedge \text{False} = \text{False}. The second branch checks \text{DirectedEdge}(A_{\mathrm{ex}}, C) \wedge \text{DirectedEdge}(A_d, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) = \text{False}. The third branch checks \text{DirectedEdge}(A_{\mathrm{ex}}, C) \wedge \text{DirectedEdge}(A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d) = \text{False}. The fourth branch checks \text{DirectedEdge}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) \wedge \text{DirectedEdge}(A_d, A_{\mathrm{ex}}) = \text{True}. Hence \text{classifyTriplet}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d) = \text{Collider}. \square

Lemma C.2 (Collider is open when A_{\mathrm{ex}} \in Z). When Z = [A_{\mathrm{ex}}],

\text{IsTripletBlocked}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{false}

Proof. By Lemma C.1, the triplet is a collider. The blocking rule for a collider is:

\text{IsTripletBlocked}(u, v, w, Z) = \neg(v \in Z \vee \exists d \in \text{Desc}(v) : d \in Z)

Evaluate v = A_{\mathrm{ex}}. We have A_{\mathrm{ex}} \in [A_{\mathrm{ex}}], so

\text{InZOrDescendantInZ}(A_{\mathrm{ex}}, [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{True}

Therefore,

\text{IsTripletBlocked}(C, A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \neg \text{True} = \text{false}

\square

Lemma C.3 (First recursive step yields open path). For the path \pi_{\text{collider}} and Z = [A_{\mathrm{ex}}],

\text{IsPathBlocked}(\pi_{\text{collider}}, [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{IsPathBlocked}([A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}])

Proof. By the definition of IsPathBlocked and Lemma C.2, the first triplet is not blocked, so the recursion proceeds with the tail. \square

Proof of Theorem 1. Assume, for contradiction, that

\text{DSeparated}([C], [H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \Pi)

holds. By definition, for every path \pi \in \Pi with endpoints C and H,

\text{IsPathBlocked}(\pi, [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{true}

Apply this to \pi_{\text{collider}} \in \Pi, which has endpoints C and H. Hence

\text{IsPathBlocked}(\pi_{\text{collider}}, [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{true}

By Lemma C.3, this equals

\text{IsPathBlocked}([A_{\mathrm{ex}}, A_d, G, H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}]) = \text{true}

By Lemma C.2, the first triplet is not blocked, so the equation cannot hold. Contradiction. Therefore,

\neg \, \text{DSeparated}([C], [H], [A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \Pi)

\square

Corollary C.4 (Endogenous selection bias). Estimating the effect of C on H while conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} induces spurious association between C and G.

Proof. Conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} opens the collider path C \to A_{\mathrm{ex}} \leftarrow A_d \leftarrow G. By Theorem 1, C and H are d-connected given A_{\mathrm{ex}}. The spurious association between C and G propagates through the path to H. \square

