Appendix E. Proof of Theorem 3: Sequential Ignorability

Theorem 3 (Sequential ignorability for mediation via A_{\mathrm{ex}}). Given the adjustment set

Z_{\text{med}} = [C, G, E, A_{\mathrm{ex}}]

the direct effect of A_d on H is identified in the sense that

\text{DSeparated}([A_d], [H], [C, G, E, A_{\mathrm{ex}}], \Pi)

Lemma E.1 (All parents of A_{\mathrm{ex}} are conditioned). The parents of A_{\mathrm{ex}} in the TLSACE graph are A_d, C, R. The adjustment set Z_{\text{med}} contains A_d (via the conditioning) and C. The vertex R is assumed to affect H only through B and thus does not create a backdoor path to H.

Lemma E.2 (Backdoor paths from A_d to H are blocked). Every path from A_d to H that begins with an arrow into A_d passes through either G or A_{\mathrm{ex}}. Conditioning on G \in Z_{\text{med}} blocks the path through G. Conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} \in Z_{\text{med}} blocks the path through A_{\mathrm{ex}}.

Lemma E.3 (Direct path A_d \to A_{\mathrm{ex}} \to H is blocked). Conditioning on A_{\mathrm{ex}} \in Z_{\text{med}} blocks the direct path.

Proof of Theorem 3. By Lemmas E.1, E.2, and E.3, all paths from A_d to H are blocked given Z_{\text{med}}. \square

Remark E.4 (Strength of the assumption). Sequential ignorability is a strong assumption. It requires that the mediator A_{\mathrm{ex}} is ignorable given observed confounders, and that the outcome H is ignorable given the mediator and confounders. This cannot be verified from data alone. Every report using this decomposition must state the assumption explicitly.

