Appendix I. Hypothesis Set with Falsification Conditions

The following hypotheses are the falsifiable claims of TLSACE. They are stated with their falsification conditions.

I.1 H1: Capability does not uniquely determine exploration

Statement. Systems with comparable cognitive capabilities may exhibit substantially different exploration behavior when their objectives differ.

Formalization. Let C_1, C_2 be two capability profiles with \|C_1 - C_2\| < \delta, and let O_1 \neq O_2 be two objective configurations. Then there exists \epsilon > 0 such that

|B(C_1, O_1) - B(C_2, O_2)| > \epsilon

Falsification condition. Under a well-specified range of controlled objectives, capability differences consistently explain exploration choices while objective variations have negligible effects, with adequate statistical power and measurement validity.

I.2 H2: Behavioral curiosity can occur without demonstrated subjective curiosity

Statement. An artificial system may systematically select actions that increase useful information without providing evidence sufficient to establish subjective experience.

Formalization. There exists a policy \pi such that B(\pi) is high (information-seeking is frequent and effective) but no behavioral test distinguishes \pi from a non-curious policy except through the information-seeking behavior itself.

Falsification condition. Every system that exhibits behavioral curiosity also exhibits a distinguishing behavioral signature that is sufficient to establish phenomenal curiosity (which would contradict the methodological premise).

I.3 H3: Operational autonomy does not necessarily reduce cooperation

Statement. Increasing the number of decisions made without direct human intervention does not necessarily reduce cooperation if authorization boundaries, objectives, and accountability remain effective.

Formalization. Let A_{\mathrm{ex}} increase by \Delta A_{\mathrm{ex}} > 0. Then \mathbf{H} may remain stable or improve provided G is in the enhanced regime.

Falsification condition. Across adequately controlled and replicated experiments, increased autonomy consistently degrades predefined cooperation outcomes despite interventions intended to preserve cooperation.

I.4 H4: Objective conflict predicts cooperation failure better than capability alone

Statement. When a system's operational objectives conflict with agreed human constraints, cooperation failures may increase even if cognitive capability remains unchanged.

Formalization. The effect size of O on H_c (compliance) exceeds the effect size of C on H_c in the conflict regime.

Falsification condition. Capability alone predicts compliance failures better than objective conflict in matched comparisons.

I.5 H5: Governance moderates capability-related risks

Statement. Authorization controls, verifiable reporting, bounded permissions, and mechanisms for suspending or reviewing actions may reduce particular cooperation failures as capability and autonomy increase.

Formalization. The interaction term \gamma_{CG} in the model

H_{c} = \gamma_0 + \gamma_C C + \gamma_G G + \gamma_{CG} C G + \ldots

is negative and significant, indicating that governance attenuates the effect of capability on compliance failures.

Falsification condition. Governance does not attenuate capability-related risks, or governance introduces new failure modes that outweigh its benefits.

I.6 H6: Exploration and cooperation can coexist

Statement. There exist at least some experimentally realizable conditions under which an agent can conduct sustained autonomous scientific exploration while satisfying predefined cooperation requirements.

Formalization. There exists a configuration (C, O, G, R, E) such that B > B_{\min} (exploration is substantial) and \mathbf{H} \geq \mathbf{H}_{\min} componentwise (cooperation requirements are satisfied).

Falsification condition. No qualifying configuration is found within the specified experimental domain and resource limits. Such a result would challenge the tested configurations, but would not establish that coexistence is impossible in every conceivable system.

