Appendix Q. Open Problems and Future Work

The following problems remain open at the time of writing.

Q.1 Complete descendant computation. The function InZOrDescendantInZ in v0.5 contains a placeholder (sorry) for the transitive closure computation. A complete implementation must compute \text{Desc}(v) via the finite edge set. This is a straightforward extension of Lemma B.4.

Q.2 Derivation of identification constraints from d-separation in a unified library. While v0.5 derives Theorems 1–3 from the DAG topology, the formalization is currently independent of Causalean. A future version should import Causalean.Graph.DAG and reuse its Bayes-Ball reachability implementation.

Q.3 Semantic equivalence of d-separation. A recent result establishes that d-separation coincides with a semantic definition inspired by non-interference. Integrating this equivalence into TLSACE would strengthen the foundational justification for the identification constraints.

Q.4 Empirical validation. The factorial experiment described in §7 of v0.5 has not been implemented or run. No empirical claims can be made until the protocol is executed and the results are reported.

Q.5 Systematic literature review. A PRISMA-compliant systematic review is required before any claim of novelty can be advanced. The current literature mapping is initial and provisional.

Q.6 Integration of game-theoretic stability conditions. The 2026 analysis of AGI decision-theoretic models proves that stable peace requires negative betrayal incentive. Integrating this condition into the TLSACE governance variables is a natural extension.

Q.7 Extrapolation limits. No formal characterization of which mechanisms tested on current agents are likely to persist in future superintelligences has been established. This is an open conceptual problem.

Q.8 Alternative graph structures. The TLSACE DAG assumes a specific topology. Alternative topologies (e.g., with A_{\mathrm{ex}} affecting G through feedback, or with C affecting G) may produce different identification results. A systematic exploration of topological variants is warranted.

